mpic matches and then  managed to get holyfield to sign it too  when bill presents the double  autographed relic at the gold and silver  pawn shop he wants 500 for it corey  harrison calls in steve gratt the  principal authenticator at beckett  authentication services to verify the  item after analyzing the ink and  comparing the signatures to other old  tyson and holyfield autographs grad  declares the stub a winner and stakes  its value around two thousand dollars  while corey offers bill one thousand  dollars for the item the two guys reach  an impasse and bill walks away without  selling deciding to frame the ticket  instead  in a 2020 episode a man named dennis was  looking to sell off an old  well-preserved long gun one that leaves  rick and corey harrison scratching their  heads over its true worth corey is only  able to identify the french origins of  the firearm whereas rick immediately  realizes they have a boutique rifle on  their hands describing the gunsmith as a  rembrandt of guns and the first gunsmith  to really combine function and artistry  rick elaborates that boutique catered to  the wealthiest customers in the late  1700s and early 1800s dennis wants ten  thousand dollars for his treasure but  before offering any amount the  harrison's call in alex cranmer ceo of  international military antiques to  assess the value a visibly excited  crammer describes boute as the official  gunsmith of french king louis xvi and  later emperor napoleon after admiring  the rifles detail and ornamentation and  giving it a try on a shooting range he  estimates his potential auction selling  price at thirty thousand dollars dennis  ups his asking price to twenty thousand  dollars but rick eventually talks him  down to sixteen thousand dollars i now  own a fine boutain not that i haven't  always had one it took you 50 something  years to get one  in a season 10 episode of pawn stars a  guy named john enters the gold and  silver pond looking to sell off an item  that 