ation jody persuades rick to pay  him 11 000 for it  probably the most famous part of the  1960 alfred hitchcock film cycle is the  iconic scene in which a criminal on the  lamb played by janet lee is brutally  murdered in a motel shower by the  disturbed norman bates portrayed by  anthony perkins the production certainly  went through a few bathroom related  props like shower curtains and a plastic  one found its way into the gold and  silver pawn shop on a season 13 episode  of pawn stars it's kind of creepy it's  even creepier  because it's signed by anthony perkins  the basketball player a man named mark  explained that he wanted in a contest in  san diego and got it autographed by  perkins in blood red ink after he gave a  speech about directing cycle 3 in 1986  mark ideally wants 400 to part with the  curtain but won't go any lower than 300  those terms seem reasonable to rick  harrison but to confirm its value he  calls in authenticator steve gratt the  expert confirms that the item was indeed  signed by perkins he puts its value in  the area of eight hundred dollars to one  thousand dollars more than double mark's  asking price after a little haggling  rick agrees to buy it from mark for 500  robbie the robot became one of the most  familiar and iconic mechanical men in  all of pop culture after its first  appearance in the 1956 sci-fi movie  forbidden planet designed in 1955 for  mgm by artist robert kinoshita robbie  would eventually appear in more than two  dozen movies and tv shows including the  twilight zone and the addams family a  client named stanley pops into the gold  and silver pawn shop carrying a robbie  the robot toy from the mid 20th century  still in its original box as stanley  explains to corey harrison and chumley  the toy procured at an estate sale was  produced in japan and is labeled  mechanized robot because mgm wouldn't  license out the rights to make name  brand replicas stanley wants two  thousand dollars for the toy in box but  skeptical of its authenticity corey  brings in johnny an expert on old toys  and owner of the toy shop johnny likes  what he sees the robot still works and  his battery compartment doesn't show any  signs of corrosion citing the big  secondary marker for such nostalgic toys  johnny figures the robot's value at 2  200  more than stanley's dream price after  some countering harrison buys the toy  for eleven hundred dollars  a young man named kevin heads into the  gold and silver pawn shop on a season 17  episode to inquire about selling a  helmet he bought at an estate cell the  former football player thought the item  was reminiscent of a simple helmet from  the sport's early days but after some  research found out that it's probably an  iron battle helmet from the anglo-saxon  era of european history chumley agrees  that the metal helmet could be up to  1400 years old made from thin iron and  weighing between five to eight pounds  the helmet is called the spang and helm  german for metal strip helmet kevin  hopes to get 750 for the piece but since  chumlee has no idea what it's worth he  brings in bob dodge of artemis testing  lab dodge notes how remarkable it is  that the thing lasted for more than a  millennium without oxidizing into dust  he uses an x-ray gun to determine that  it's made from the error-specific carbon  steel and suggests the one in a million  item belongs in a museum and it's worth  about fifteen thousand dollars chumley  and kevin ultimately brought her down to  ten thousand dollars  a woman named april seeks to sell an old  relic needing money for moving expenses  and a big screen tv she knows she's got  something pretty valuable during her  visit to the gold and silver pawn shop  in a 2010 episode presenting her 1602  shipwrecked dutch east india company  bell to rick and his father old man  richard harrison richard is instantly  skeptical that the item is worth much or  even came from a shipwreck claiming it  lacks enough corrosion to have been in  salt water for any lengthy period of  time it would have been good for  business had the harrisons made a  nominal offer for the bell since after  they called in their expert carl to  evaluate the bell they determined it to  be a genuine relic of the dutch east  india company which dominated world  trade for over two centuries carl  explains ninety percent of shipwrecks  occur in shallow water and it is highly  likely natives easily removed this bell  and brought it ashore citing its  worthiness of being in a treasure  auction catalog carl cites a cash value  of fifteen thousand dollars while  richard only offers a measly three  thousand dollars for the bell rick  eventually haggles with april to settle  at 7 200  while the house of faberge famous for  its faberge eggs was the most  prestigious jeweler in 1800s imperial  russia its main competitor was the  equally impressive house of bowlin  according to craig gottlieb of craig  gottlieb military antiques that's who  manufactured a medal that came across  the display cases at the gold and silver  pawn shop in a 2014 episode rick  harrison offers a substantial sum of six  thousand dollars to the client who  brought in the white eagle medallion  thought to be a centuries-old  gem-studded polish piece gottlieb  explains it's not really polish but  russian in 1795 poland was partitioned  into segments ruled by prussia austria  and russia and the latter took the white  eagle symbol of a polish order founded  in 1325 and combined with the russian  two-headed eagle it's a really neat i  mean this is 800 years of history here  this medal shows just how hard poland's  had it for the past 800 years that leaf  says the medallion was likely forged in  saint petersburg and that it's worth  thirty thousand dollars to forty  thousand dollars harrison's original  offer for the medal was far under its  actual worth and the new estimate forces  him to offer a lot more than he expected  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  pawn stars are coming soon subscribe to  our youtube channel and hit the bell so  you don't miss a single one  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

